<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROBE C3. NEGATIVE CONTROL for C1/C2, and the file that answers section 13.8's
  named gap. Byte-for-byte C1/C2's model, except that NOBODY declares the
  outer-to-inner edge:

      result -> outer   declared on the decision
      outer  -> inner   NOT DECLARED ANYWHERE, though outer's body calls inner

  Section 6.2 says the knowledgeRequirement is "load bearing and unenforced by
  the XSD; omit it and KIE errors at both compile and eval time", which section
  13.3 row 5 measured at DECISION level. Section 13.8 records that the Camunda
  side of the same question was never asked and says "Worth a probe before
  Phase 5 ships BKM." This is that probe.

  EXPECTED KIE: loud. This fixture is wired HarnessMustFail on the KIE leg; a
  day when it starts passing is a day the emitter lost its engine backstop
  without anyone noticing.

  WHAT THE CAMUNDA LEG MEANS. If Camunda 8 is SILENT here (parses, evaluates,
  answers the right number), then on the DEFAULT flavor section 6.2's invariant
  is checked by nobody but us, and a checkDrg assertion over knowledgeRequirement
  completeness becomes MANDATORY rather than belt-and-braces. If Camunda is also
  loud, the assertion is still worth having but is no longer the only guard.

  The cases assert the CORRECT answer deliberately, not an error: if an engine
  answers correctly without the edge, the value leg passes and the difference
  between the engines shows up in the error count, which is exactly the
  distinction being measured.

  MEASURED 2026-07-31, answering section 13.8's open question:

    KIE 8.44  : ERROR [ERR_COMPILING_FEEL] ... Unknown variable 'inner', at
                validator AND build. Loud, at compile time, exactly as section 6.2
                claims and section 13.3 row 5 measured at decision level.
    Camunda 8 : parses cleanly, no message, and `result` is NULL in every case.

  So section 13.8's worry is HALF confirmed. Camunda does not accept the file
  and answer correctly, which would have been the worst case; but it does not
  DIAGNOSE anything either. It degrades to null, which the harness catches only
  because a SUCCEEDED-yet-null value is a failure by the harness's own rule, and
  which a production deployment would experience as a decision that quietly
  stopped having an answer.

  CONSEQUENCE. On the DEFAULT flavor there is no compile-time backstop for
  knowledgeRequirement completeness. A checkDrg assertion over it is MANDATORY,
  not belt-and-braces. Wired HarnessMustFail on both legs.

  Note that this file and bkm-chain-flat.dmn (C2) produce the IDENTICAL
  diagnostic on both engines, which is itself the finding: adding the transitive
  edge to the caller changes nothing, so C2 is C3 with a decoration.

-->
<definitions xmlns="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20191111/MODEL/" id="definitions_bkm_chain_nokr" name="bkm_chain_nokr" namespace="https://legalese.com/l4/dmn/bkm_chain_nokr" exporter="hand">
  <businessKnowledgeModel id="bkm_inner" name="inner">
    <variable id="bkm_inner_var" name="inner" typeRef="number"/>
    <encapsulatedLogic id="bkm_inner_logic">
      <formalParameter id="bkm_inner_p1" name="v" typeRef="number"/>
      <literalExpression id="bkm_inner_lit">
        <text>v + 1</text>
      </literalExpression>
    </encapsulatedLogic>
  </businessKnowledgeModel>
  <businessKnowledgeModel id="bkm_outer" name="outer">
    <variable id="bkm_outer_var" name="outer" typeRef="number"/>
    <encapsulatedLogic id="bkm_outer_logic">
      <formalParameter id="bkm_outer_p1" name="v" typeRef="number"/>
      <literalExpression id="bkm_outer_lit">
        <text>inner(v) * 10</text>
      </literalExpression>
    </encapsulatedLogic>
  </businessKnowledgeModel>
  <inputData id="input_n" name="n">
    <variable id="input_n_var" name="n" typeRef="number"/>
  </inputData>
  <decision id="decision_result" name="result">
    <variable id="decision_result_var" name="result" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_result_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_result_outer">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#bkm_outer"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_result_lit">
      <text>outer(n)</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
</definitions>
